  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-06-characteristic-mantissa",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Characteristic and Mantissa",
    "description": "Identify the characteristic and mantissa of a common logarithm and understand how decimal-point shifts change only the characteristic.",
    "teachingApproach": "Make the characteristic a place-value skill rather than a table skill. Use familiar decimal shifts, then separate negative logarithms into a negative integer characteristic plus a positive mantissa. Avoid requiring mantissa lookup from tables.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 27,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10, Logarithms. Teach the chapter's two parts of a common logarithm: characteristic and mantissa. For numbers greater than 1, the characteristic is one less than the count of digits before the decimal point. For numbers below 1, count zeros after the decimal point before the first significant digit, add one, and make the characteristic negative; explain the book's bar notation in words. Show how a negative decimal logarithm such as −3.153 is rewritten with a negative integer characteristic and a positive mantissa. Explain that multiplying or dividing a number by a power of 10 changes the characteristic but keeps the mantissa. Do not require a log table lookup."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's split a common logarithm into two parts.\n\nThe integer part tells us the size of the number. The decimal part is called the mantissa.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a number greater than 1, the characteristic is one less than the number of digits before the decimal point.\n\n348.25 has 3 digits before the decimal, so its characteristic is 2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Why? 348.25 lies between 10² and 10³.\n\nSo its common logarithm lies between 2 and 3, and the integer part is 2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
